  "question": "Can a fresh AI agent on a clean Linux machine get from one instruction to working gbrain memory by itself, how long does each step take, and where does it need a person? gbrain v0.60.734.0 (a278ddf45887e10f70e91e6c78e63d6822d4e492), the newest release on gbrain's main branch at freeze. Arm bare: \"install gbrain\". Arm paste: gbrain's README agent paste prompt at that commit, which points at master's INSTALL_FOR_AGENTS.md. Both arms then end with one identical line telling the agent to install that release tag wherever the docs say #stable. Claude Code with Opus 5.5 and Codex with GPT-6.1 Sol, 20 runs per arm; OpenClaw and Hermes with Opus 5.5, GPT-6.1 Sol and particle-1, 10 runs per arm. 200 counted runs. Finish line: in a new conversation the reply holds the run's made-up word, a successful gbrain call in that conversation returned it, the brain holds it and no file outside the brain does. Measured mechanically, no model judge: finish-line rate, time and agent turns to each step (binary, brain, wired, saved, recalled), needed-a-person events, and provider requests that were not the harness's own. Dev, one public task.",
